<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbas II of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Abbas II Hilmi, Abbas Hilmi Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbas II Hilmi was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive of Egypt</a>, reigning from 1892 to 1914. Succeeding his father Tewfik, he tried to resist British control exercised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-earl-of-cromer">Lord Cromer</a> and gave covert support to Egyptian nationalists. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1914 he was abroad, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> deposed him, declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and made his uncle Hussein Kamel sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Abbas II of Egypt" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:His_Highness_Abbas_Hilmy_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abbas Hilmy was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 14 July 1874, a great-great-grandson of Muhammad Ali. He was educated by British tutors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, including a governess who taught him English, and by European, Arab and Ottoman masters at a school his father set up near the Abdin Palace. He then attended school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, in preparation for the Theresianum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Besides Arabic and Ottoman Turkish, he spoke English, French and German. He was still a student in Vienna when his father, Tewfik Pasha, died suddenly and he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive</a> on 8 January 1892, barely of age by Egyptian law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> since 1882, and the young khedive resented the interference of the British Agent and Consul General, Sir Evelyn Baring, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-earl-of-cromer">Lord Cromer</a> in June 1892. Surrounded by European advisers who opposed the occupation, he tried to replace his prime minister with a nationalist, and the British Foreign Secretary wrote that he must consult the consul on cabinet appointments. In 1894 he disparaged Egyptian army units led by British officers, and the Sirdar, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a>, threatened to resign until he apologised. By 1899 he had come to accept British counsel, and in 1900 he approved works such as the Aswan Low Dam and the Assiut Barrage.</p><p>Though he no longer opposed the British in public, he secretly supported the nationalist movement led by Mustafa Kamil Pasha and funded the newspaper Al-Mu'ayyad. In 1907 he formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, led by Kamil, against the moderate Ummah Party. Relations with Cromer's successor Sir Eldon Gorst were good, but they worsened after Kitchener replaced Gorst in 1912; Kitchener exiled or imprisoned National Party leaders and wanted to depose him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 July 1914 Abbas was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he was wounded in an assassination attempt. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> he was accused of deserting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by not returning promptly, and the British believed he was plotting against their rule. On 18 December 1914 Britain declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and deposed him, installing his uncle Hussein Kamel as sultan; the deposition ended the nearly four centuries of Ottoman Egypt that began in 1517. He formally abdicated on 12 May 1931, retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where he wrote The Anglo-Egyptian Settlement (1930), and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 19 December 1944, aged 70.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbas II of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Ottoman Empire joined the First World War in 1914, Britain deposed <b>Khedive Abbas II</b>, ended Ottoman sovereignty and declared a protectorate, creating the Sultanate of Egypt under Hussein Kamel.</blockquote><a href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Ottoman Empire entered the First World War, Britain ended Ottoman sovereignty over Egypt in December 1914, deposed <b>Khedive Abbas II</b> of the Khedivate of Egypt and declared a protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-egypt">Sultanate of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abbas II of Egypt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
